   "A": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the real property tax law, in relation to requiring notification to be given to the local housing agency in the event that cessation of construction on a project for which an application for a certain tax exemption has been made equals or exceeds one hundred twenty days",
    "basePrintNo": "A8125",
    "basePrintNoStr": "A8125-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §421-a, RPT L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Tax Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A8125A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the real property tax law, in relation\nto requiring notification to be given to the local housing agency in the\nevent that cessation of construction on a project for which an applica-\ntion for a certain tax exemption has been made equals or exceeds one\nhundred twenty days\n\nPURPOSE: To require applicants under the 421-a tax abatement program to\nprovide written notice to the local housing agency when construction\nceases for a period exceeding one hundred twenty days.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:  Section 1: Section 421-a of the real property\ntax law is amended by adding a new subdivision 5-a that requires appli-\ncants to affirmatively notify the local housing agency in writing when\nconstruction ceases for a period exceeding one hundred twenty days, the\nreasons for the cessation and the date construction is expected to\nresume.\n\nSection 2: Effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION: It is important for communities and local housing agen-\ncies to be informed about construction projects that require 421-a tax\nabatements coming to a halt. This notification will be an alert that\nexpected affordable housing is no longer imminent.  Furthermore,\nprojects that cease construction without explanation leave neighbors\nquestioning whether the excavation in their midst will ever conclude and\nprovide the needed housing and improvements that were promised when the\nproject was approved. Requiring the developer to provide notice when\nconstruction ceases, and estimate when it will resume, will help assuage\nthese concerns and allow local housing agencies to help bring affordable\nhousing on line.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY: New bill in the Assembly; Same as S5281 of 2009\n(Squadron).\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: None.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: This act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
